            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> When sending \"trash\" to stdpath('data'), if a file with the same name already exists, the new one will not overwrite the old one. May I check if this is work as design?\r\n\r\nIt is [explicitly ensured](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/71b5694a2df4f7e3661855eb8e2814980d2e0ac9/lua/mini/files.lua#L2581-L2586) that before file/directory is moved to the \"trash directory\" (`stdpath('data')` + '/mini.files/trash'), there is no file with the same basename there.\r\n\r\nLet's clarify this a bit. So the steps are:\r\n- Have 'mini.files' set up with `require('mini.files').setup({ options = { permanent_delete = false } })`.\r\n- Create a file 'aaa' with content \"This is old file\".\r\n- Delete it (move to trash) with 'mini.files'. There now should be file named 'aaa' in \"trash directory\"  with content \"This is old file\".\r\n- Again create a file 'aaa' with content 'This is new file'.\r\n- Delete it (move to trash) with 'mini.files'.\r\n\r\nAfter these steps I have a file named 'aaa' in \"trash directory\" with content \"This is new file\". I.e. it has replaced the previous one. Do you observe a different behavior?",
            "created_at": "2024-08-20T13:02:37Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1152#issuecomment-2298809236",
            "id": 2298809236,
